<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manitoba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manitoba is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, with Winnipeg as its capital. It was created in 1870 from part of Rupert's Land after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-resistance">Red River Resistance</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>, whose provisional government negotiated its entry into Confederation, and was enlarged northwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manitoba.jpg" alt="Manitoba" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kooma (original) Echando una mano (current) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Manitoba.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Manitoba lies at the longitudinal centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nunavut">Nunavut</a> and the Northwest Territories to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> to the northeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> to the east, North Dakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> to the west. Its landscape runs from arctic tundra and the Hudson Bay coast in the north to boreal forest, large freshwater lakes and prairie in the centre and south. It lies at the centre of the Hudson Bay drainage basin, where much of the water drains into Lake Winnipeg and then north down the Nelson River. Most of the inhabited south developed in the bed of the prehistoric Glacial Lake Agassiz, and the Red River Valley is flat and fertile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>First Nations peoples settled the area about 10,000 years ago, shortly after the last ice age glaciers retreated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a>, Dene, Sioux, Mandan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a> founded settlements, and the first farming was along the Red River, where corn and other seed crops were planted before contact with Europeans. English and French fur traders arrived in the early 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> secured the region in 1670 as Rupert's Land under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>, and the early economy centred on the trade of beaver pelts and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-douglas-5th-earl-of-selkirk">Lord Selkirk</a> brought the first agricultural settlers in 1811, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a> of Indigenous and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis people</a> grew up.</p><p>Deep disagreements over self-determination during the transfer of Rupert's Land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1869 led to armed resistance under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>, who established a provisional government. Its assembly sent three delegates to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>, and the negotiations produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba-act">Manitoba Act</a>, introduced by Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">Sir John A. Macdonald</a>, which made Manitoba the fifth province of Canada in 1870. The name was chosen by Thomas Spence, and Riel preferred it to the alternative Assiniboia. Riel fled into exile, and the Canadian government blocked the Métis' attempts to obtain the land promised to them. Facing racism from a flood of settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, many Métis moved to what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>.</p><p>By 1911 Winnipeg was the third largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Nellie McClung led the campaign for women's votes, and on 28 January 1916 Manitoba became the first province to allow women to vote in provincial elections, two years before Canada did so nationally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Manitoba is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> with Winnipeg as its capital and largest city, home to nearly two thirds of the provincial population in its metropolitan region. English and French are both official languages of its legislature and courts under the Manitoba Act, a status the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-court-of-canada">Supreme Court of Canada</a> upheld in 1985. The population was 1,342,153 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The Winnipeg-based Royal Winnipeg Ballet, granted its royal title in 1953, is Canada's oldest ballet company.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manitoba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Macdonald, prime minister in 1867–1873 and 1878–1891, expanded the dominion by acquiring Rupert’s Land and the North-Western Territory from the Hudson’s Bay Company in 1870, creating <b>Manitoba</b>; British Columbia joined in 1871 on the promise of a railway, and Prince Edward Island in 1873.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…five Canadian provinces before 1896, which led to the idea that it was made at the time of the Wolseley Expedition of 1870 to the Red River Valley of <b>Manitoba</b>, as the lament of a local woman, perhaps a Métis, whose soldier lover was about to return east.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-river-valley">Red River Valley</a></li><li><blockquote>Promised land for the Métis was largely lost, but Riel is now honoured in <b>Manitoba</b> as its founder.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a></li><li><blockquote>Negotiations led to the creation of <b>Manitoba</b> as a province of Canada in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/state/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>The grant covered much of what is now northern Quebec and Ontario, <b>Manitoba</b>, Saskatchewan, Alberta and parts of the northern United States, though the company knew little of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a></li></ul></section>
